=begin
  Collects insects from a fixed circuit of Crossing-area rooms.

  Walks the circuit until a bug is in the room, picks it up, and places it on the
  shrine in room 10506. Wings are dropped. Waits and re-walks when nothing spawns.
  Stops with a message when a walk fails.
=end

# Every room must be reachable from every other in the current mapdb, and
# Lich must be able to tell it apart from every other room, since a failed walk
# stops the script. walk_to only reports arrival when Room.current is the
# target, and Lich takes a room the map has no uid for to be the first room in
# the map with the same title, description and exits. Many Lowest Depths corridors
# look exactly alike, so the circuit leaves out each one that shares its look
# with a lower-numbered room; walks still pass through them.
rooms = [10_506, 10_507, 10_508, 10_509, 10_482, 10_497, 10_515, 10_510, 10_539, 10_590, 10_520, 10_640, 10_557, 10_576, 10_617, 10_620,
         10_572, 10_677]

# Whether a noun is one of the bug nouns, singular or plural (crickets,
# mantises, fireflies). The whole noun has to be the bug word: DR matches
# `get the bug` against the start of an item's noun, so a bugle, mothballs,
# ladybugs or a humbug must not count. The game sends some nouns with a
# leading space.
#
# @param noun [String, nil] an object's noun
# @return [Boolean]
def bug_noun?(noun)
  noun.to_s.strip.match?(/\A(?:(?:locust|bug|beetle|cricket|hornet|moth|scarab|mantis)(?:e?s)?|(?:fire|dragon)fl(?:y|ies))\z/i)
end

# The first bug in the room, as what to get and the name to report it by, or
# nil when nothing in the room is a bug. Gets already tried and failed in this
# room are passed in and skipped, so an object that only looks like a bug
# can't keep the room's real bugs from being picked up.
#
# An object the room's links name (GameObj.loot) is a bug when the game's own
# noun for it is a bug noun, and is got by its id. A vela'tohr bush surrounded
# by ephemeral flickering fireflies is a bush, and a moth antenna or the
# dropped dragonfly wing is no bug. Only when no linked object is a bug does
# the room's object list (DRRoom.room_objs) count: the game sends that list
# without links, or cuts it short with links for the objects before the cut.
#
# @param missed [Array<String>] gets that took nothing in this room
# @return [Array(String, String), nil] what to get (`#<id>` or
#   `the <adjective> <noun>`) and the bug's name
def room_bug(missed = [])
  loot = GameObj.loot.to_a
  bug = loot.find { |obj| bug_noun?(obj.noun) && !missed.include?("##{obj.id}") }
  return ["##{bug.id}", bug.name] if bug

  listed_room_bug(loot.map(&:name), missed)
end

# The first bug in the room's object list that the room's links don't name,
# as `the <adjective> <noun>` for the get and `<adjective> <noun>` to report
# it by, or nil when there is none. With no links, lich-5's DRC.get_noun gives
# each object's noun. The word before the noun goes into the get as well, so
# `get the stink bug` can't take a battered brass bugle listed ahead of the
# bug. Both words are used as the room lists them; an object listed under its
# noun alone, or after an article, is named by its noun alone.
#
# get_noun reads the bush above as fireflies, so such an object is tried once
# per visit, takes nothing, and is passed over for the next bug.
#
# A linked object is left out only when the room's object list gives it under
# the same whole name. lich-5 splits that list at commas and at " and ", so a
# linked object whose name holds either is listed in pieces, and each piece
# is judged by DRC.get_noun like an unlinked object: at worst one get that
# takes nothing.
#
# @param linked [Array<String>] names of the objects the room's links name,
#   whose real nouns room_bug has already judged
# @param missed [Array<String>] gets that took nothing in this room
# @return [Array(String, String), nil]
def listed_room_bug(linked, missed)
  DRRoom.room_objs.each do |obj|
    name = obj.sub(/\A(?:a|an|some|the)\s+/i, '')
    next if linked.include?(name)

    noun = DRC.get_noun(name)
    next unless bug_noun?(noun)

    words = name.split
    at = words.index { |word| word.casecmp?(noun) }
    bug = [(words[at - 1] if at&.positive?), noun].compact.join(' ')
    return ["the #{bug}", bug] unless missed.include?("the #{bug}")
  end
  nil
end

# Walks to a room on the circuit or to the shrine. When the walk fails, stops
# the script instead of searching the wrong rooms or trying to put the bug on a
# shrine that isn't there. A false from walk_to while standing in the room
# still counts as arrived: some lich-5 releases return false after a one-step
# walk out of a room they couldn't identify, even though the step reached the
# target.
def walk_to_or_stop(room, held = nil)
  return if DRCT.walk_to(room)
  return if Room.current&.id == room

  DRC.message("bug-grabber: could not reach room #{room}. Stopping.")
  DRC.message("bug-grabber: the #{held} is still in your hands.") if held
  exit
end

# What each hand holds, as GameObj shows it (right hand first), or nil for a
# hand that holds nothing.
#
# @return [Array(Object, Object)] the right and left hands' GameObj, each nil
#   when empty
def held_items
  [GameObj.right_hand, GameObj.left_hand].map do |hand|
    hand if hand && !['', 'Empty'].include?(hand.name.to_s)
  end
end

# Whether two GameObj hand items are the same object: same id and same name.
#
# @param item [Object, nil] a hand's GameObj
# @param other [Object, nil] a hand's GameObj
# @return [Boolean]
def same_item?(item, other)
  !item.nil? && !other.nil? && item.id == other.id && item.name == other.name
end

# What a get picked up: the item in the first hand (right, then left) that
# holds something it did not hold before the get, with that hand's place in
# held_items. Nil when neither hand changed, so something already held is
# never taken for the bug.
#
# @param before [Array(Object, Object)] held_items from before the get
# @return [Array(Object, Integer), nil] the item got and its hand's index
def picked_up(before)
  held_items.each_with_index.find { |item, hand| item && !same_item?(item, before[hand]) }
end

# Picks up each bug in the room in turn, drops a wing and takes anything else
# to the shrine. Only what the get picked up is handled: the hands are read
# before and after the get, and the hand that holds something new is the one
# the get filled. What goes on the shrine is named by the noun GameObj gives
# for that item, and it is stowed after only when that hand still holds it.
# Anything the character already held stays in hand. A get that puts nothing
# new in either hand (the object can't be picked up, someone else got it
# first, both hands were full, or it only looked like a bug) leaves the
# object where it is, and the next bug in the room is tried. Returns false
# when nothing was picked up, so the circuit moves on instead of coming
# straight back for an object it can't get.
#
# @return [Boolean] whether anything was picked up
def collect_bugs
  missed = []
  took = false
  while (bug = room_bug(missed))
    target, name = bug
    fput('stand') until checkstanding
    before = held_items
    fput("get #{target}")
    pause 1
    waitrt?
    item, hand = picked_up(before)
    if item.nil?
      DRC.message("bug-grabber: could not pick up the #{name}. Moving on.")
      missed << target
      next
    end
    thing = item.noun.to_s.strip
    if thing.match?(/\bwing/i)
      fput('drop wing')
    else
      walk_to_or_stop(10_506, thing)
      missed.clear
      fput("put #{thing} on shrine")
      pause
      waitrt?
      pause
      fput("stow #{thing}") if same_item?(held_items[hand], item)
    end
    took = true
    waitrt?
  end
  took
end

# Walks the circuit until a room's bugs are collected, then starts over. A
# circuit that collects nothing waits before the next one.
loop do
  collected = rooms.any? do |room|
    walk_to_or_stop(room)
    room_bug && collect_bugs
  end
  waitrt?
  next if collected

  pause 15
  echo 'waiting on more bugs'
end
